  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the equation of the line that is tangent to both circles \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\) and satisfies the condition that the line segment \\(P_1P_2\\) (where \\(P_1\\) is the point of tangency on \\(C_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) is the point of tangency on \\(C_2\\)) is perpendicular to the line passing through the centers of \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the equations of the circles in standard form to identify their centers and radii:\n\nFor \\(C_1: x^2 + y^2 - 2x - 4y + 1 = 0\\):\n\\[ (x-1)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 4 \\]\nSo, the center of \\(C_1\\) is \\((1, 2)\\) and the radius is \\(2\\).\n\nFor \\(C_2: x^2 + y^2 + 2x - 6y + 2 = 0\\):\n\\[ (x+1)^2 + (y-3)^2 = 8 \\]\nSo, the center of \\(C_2\\) is \\((-1, 3)\\) and the radius is \\(2\\sqrt{2}\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the equation of the line passing through the centers of \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\). The slope of this line is:\n\\[ m = \\frac{3-2}{-1-1} = -\\frac{1}{2} \\]\nSo, the equation of the line passing through \\((1, 2)\\) and \\((-1, 3)\\) is:\n\\[ y - 2 = -\\frac{1}{2}(x - 1) \\]\n\\[ 2y - 4 = -x + 1 \\]\n\\[ x + 2y = 5 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the points of tangency \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) such that the line segment \\(P_1P_2\\) is perpendicular to the line \\(x + 2y = 5\\). The slope of the line \\(x + 2y = 5\\) is \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\), so the slope of the line \\(P_1P_2\\) is \\(2\\) (since the product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is \\(-1\\)).\n\nLet's denote the points of tangency as \\(P_1(x_1, y_1)\\) on \\(C_1\\) and \\(P_2(x_2, y_2)\\) on \\(C_2\\). The slope of the tangent line at \\(P_1\\) is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the radius from the center of \\(C_1\\) to \\(P_1\\), and similarly for \\(P_2\\). The slopes of the radii are:\n\\[ \\text{slope of radius from }(1, 2) \\text{ to } P_1 = \\frac{y_1 - 2}{x_1 - 1} \\]\n\\[ \\text{slope of radius from }(-1, 3) \\text{ to } P_2 = \\frac{y_2 - 3}{x_2 + 1} \\]\n\nSince the tangents are perpendicular to these radii, the slopes of the tangents are:\n\\[ \\text{slope of tangent at } P_1 = -\\frac{x_1 - 1}{y_1 - 2} \\]\n\\[ \\text{slope of tangent at } P_2 = -\\frac{x_2 + 1}{y_2 - 3} \\]\n\nThe equation of the tangent line at \\(P_1\\) is:\n\\[ y - y_1 = -\\frac{x_1 - 1}{y_1 - 2}(x - x_1) \\]\nSince this line is also perpendicular to \\(x + 2y = 5\\), the slope of the tangent line must be \\(2\\):\n\\[ -\\frac{x_1 - 1}{y_1 - 2} = 2 \\]\n\\[ x_1 - 1 = -2(y_1 - 2) \\]\n\\[ x_1 - 1 = -2y_1 + 4 \\]\n\\[ x_1 + 2y_1 = 5 \\]\n\nSimilarly, the equation of the tangent line at \\(P_2\\) is:\n\\[ y - y_2 = -\\frac{x_2 + 1}{y_2 - 3}(x - x_2) \\]\nSince this line is also perpendicular to \\(x + 2y = 5\\), the slope of the tangent line must be \\(2\\):\n\\[ -\\frac{x_2 + 1}{y_2 - 3} = 2 \\]\n\\[ x_2 + 1 = -2(y_2 - 3) \\]\n\\[ x_2 + 1 = -2y_2 + 6 \\]\n\\[ x_2 + 2y_2 = 5 \\]\n\nWe now have the system of equations:\n\\[ x_1 + 2y_1 = 5 \\]\n\\[ x_2 + 2y_2 = 5 \\]\n\nSince \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) lie on both circles, they satisfy the equations of the circles:\n\\[ x_1^2 + y_1^2 - 2x_1 - 4y_1 + 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x_2^2 + y_2^2 + 2x_2 - 6y_2 + 2 = 0 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x_1 = 5 - 2y_1\\) and \\(x_2 = 5 - 2y_2\\) into the circle equations, we get:\n\\[ (5 - 2y_1)^2 + y_1^2 - 2(5 - 2y_1) - 4y_1 + 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ 25 - 20y_1 + 4y_1^2 + y_1^2 - 10 + 4y_1 - 4y_1 + 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ 5y_1^2 - 16y_1 + 16 = 0 \\]\n\nSolving this quadratic equation, we get:\n\\[ y_1 = \\frac{16 \\pm \\sqrt{256 - 320}}{10} = \\frac{16 \\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{10} \\]\n\nSince the discriminant is negative, there are no real solutions for \\(y_1\\), which implies that the line \\(x + 2y = 5\\) does not intersect the circles \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\) at the points of tangency \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) such that \\(P_1P_2\\) is perpendicular to the line passing through the centers of \\(C_1\\) and \\(C_2\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\text{No such line exists}} \\]",
